{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-936a5d5398099100", "type": "tool_result", "content": "200\tL\n201\tot−1 . (2)\n202\t\n203\tThe length output is monotonically calibrated to a turnboundary steering strength and, like all steering decisions,\n204\tacts through the move-level gating rule below; move-steering\n205\tstrengths are per-move constants selected on training data.\n206\tRealization-aware gating. Based on training-set steerability, move steering is withheld for reply, which the unsteered\n207\tbackbone realizes more reliably, while turn-boundary steering is applied only to naturally terse moves (acknowledge,\n208\tquery, and reply). All gates depend on the controller prediction rather than gold labels.\n209\tTraining and inference. With the backbone frozen, we\n210\ttrain the controller using next-move cross-entropy and\n211\tsmooth-L1 log-length loss. All components use the same\n212\t\n213\tdialogue-level split, excluding held-out test dialogues. At inference, the controller maintains a persistent streaming state\n214\tacross turns.\n215\t\n216\tConversational-Move Subspaces for Realization\n217\tFor each follower turn t in the training dialogues, with gold\n218\tmove yt ∈ M, we extract the residual-stream change at\n219\tresponse onset. Let hℓt (s) ∈ Rd denote the activation at layer\n220\tℓ and token position s, and let st,1 denote the position of the\n221\tfirst token of response ut . We define\n222\t∆ℓt = hℓt (st,1 ) − hℓt (st,1 − 1),\n223\t\n224\t(3)\n225\t\n226\twhich captures the transition from dialogue context to response generation. For each backbone, move directions are\n227\testimated exclusively from its activations on the MapTask\n228\ttraining split and then applied, without re-estimation, to the\n229\tsame backbone on the held-out MapTask test split, FindTask,\n230\tand CReST.\n231\tAt each layer, we standardize the onset differences\n232\t{∆ℓt }t∈Itrain , project them into a k-dimensional PCA space,\n233\tand estimate a one-versus-rest Fisher direction for each move\n234\tusing Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage (Ledoit and Wolf 2004; Fisher\n235\tℓ\n236\tℓ\n237\t1936). Let νm\n238\tand ν¬m\n239\tdenote the means of move m and\n240\tb ℓw,m denote the\n241\tall remaining moves in PCA space, and let Σ\n242\t\n243\t\fshrinkage estimate of their pooled within-class covariance.\n244\tThe discriminative direction is\n245\t\u0010\n246\t\u0011−1\n247\t\u0001\n248\tℓ\n249\tℓ\n250\tℓ\n251\tb ℓw,m\n252\twm\n253\t∝ Σ\n254\tνm\n255\t− ν¬m\n256\t.\n257\t(4)\n258\t\n259\tet al. 2025a), Qwen3-Omni (Xu et al. 2025b), and Phi-4Multimodal (Abouelenin et al. 2025).\n260\t\n261\tSelection: Predicting the Next Move\n262\t\n263\tℓ\n264\tWe map wm\n265\tback to residual-stream coordinates, undo\n266\t\n267\tthe feature scaling, and normalize the result to obtain the\n268\tℓ\n269\tmove direction vm\n270\t. The inverse covariance suppresses dimensions with high within-class variation, yielding a more\n271\tdiscriminative direction than a simple difference of means.\n272\tFor target move m, we inject its direction at the selected\n273\tlayer ℓ⋆m at every generated token position s:\n274\t⋆\n275\t\n276\tℓ⋆\n277\t\n278\t⋆\n279\t\n280\thℓm (s) ← hℓm (s) + αcℓ⋆m vmm .\n281\t(5)\n282\twhere cℓm is the median residual-stream norm at that layer\n283\tand α is a dimensionless steering coefficient. This normrelative scaling improves comparability across layers and\n284\tmodel backbones.\n285\t\n286\tAutomatic Move Classifier\n287\tWe use Qwen2.5-72B (Yang et al. 2025) as a prompted\n288\tfew-shot classifier over five move types, using the same\n289\tprompt and decoding procedure across datasets. Classification is context-dependent: we first label the preceding\n290\tgiver turn, then classify the follower response conditioned\n291\ton that label, using the gold giver label when available. Decoding is restricted to one option token, whose normalized\n292\tlog-probabilities define P (move | ·). On human corpus responses, the classifier recovers the gold move with 77.2%–\n293\t81.6% accuracy (Table 2). For generation evaluation, we\n294\twithhold the human response, generate a replacement, and\n295\tcompare its classifier-assigned move with the original human\n296\tgold label. We further validate it on 500 generated responses,\n297\tbalanced across target moves, datasets, and backbone models. Three blinded annotators label each response from its\n298\tdialogue context. The classifier agrees with their majority label on 73.9% of examples, while human agreement is Fleiss’\n299\tκ = 0.682. Across the seven generation methods, classifierbased and human-majority move accuracies are strongly correlated (Spearman ρ = 0.96) and induce nearly the same\n300\tranking, so the automatic metric does not systematically favor any particular method.\n301\t\n302\tSelector\n303\t\n304\tMapTask\n305\t\n306\tFindTask\n307\t\n308\tCReST\n309\t\n310\tAvg.\n311\t\n312\tMajority baseline\n313\tTF-IDF + logistic (last 5 turns)\n314\tGPT-4o (few-shot)\n315\tQwen2.5-72B (few-shot)\n316\tMarkov-1 (gold prev. move)\n317\tTranscript-embed. probe\n318\tTranscript-embed. SSM\n319\tActivation probe\n320\t\n321\t0.47\n322\t0.54\n323\t0.40\n324\t0.38\n325\t0.65\n326\t0.59\n327\t0.58\n328\t0.64\n329\t\n330\t0.41\n331\t0.46\n332\t0.32\n333\t0.38\n334\t0.42\n335\t0.53\n336\t0.50\n337\t0.50\n338\t\n339\t0.37\n340\t0.44\n341\t0.53\n342\t0.41\n343\t0.56\n344\t0.58\n345\t0.60\n346\t0.61\n347\t\n348\t0.42\n349\t0.48\n350\t0.42\n351\t0.39\n352\t0.54\n353\t0.57\n354\t0.56\n355\t0.58\n356\t\n357\tController (ours, streaming)\n358\t\n359\t0.62\n360\t\n361\t0.58\n362\t\n363\t0.61\n364\t\n365\t0.60\n366\t\n367\tTable 3: Next-move selection accuracy. Markov-1 receives\n368\tthe gold preceding move; the activation probe and the controller read the residual stream at the selected controller layer\n369\tℓ⋆ctrl .\n370\tThe activation controller outperforms all transcript-based selectors across datasets. On MapTask it reaches 0.62, close to\n371\tthe gold-history Markov baseline at 0.65, while on FindTask\n372\tand CReST it surpasses that baseline. The non-recurrent activation probe performs similarly on MapTask and CReST,\n373\tsuggesting that the backbone already integrates the relevant\n374\tdialogue history. On FindTask, the stateful controller improves over the probe by 0.08, indicating that explicit crossturn state is useful for shorter episodes.\n375\t\n376\tRealization: Generating a Target Move\n377\tDataset\n378\t\n379\tModel\n380\t\n381\tBase\n382\t\n383\tSI\n384\t\n385\tPBL FUDGE DeAL Ours\n386\t\n387\tQwen3\n388\t29.0 30.1 43.2\n389\tMapTask Qwen2.5 30.9 38.8 42.4\n390\tPhi-4\n391\t40.6 63.2 56.6\n392\t\n393\t29.9\n394\t30.7\n395\t46.7\n396\t\n397\t34.9\n398\t39.8\n399\t31.1\n400\t\n401\t41.0\n402\t66.8\n403\t64.8\n404\t\n405\tQwen3\n406\t29.5 34.6 46.2\n407\tFindTask Qwen2.5 23.7 25.6 34.0\n408\tPhi-4\n409\t29.5 35.3 35.9\n410\t\n411\t26.9\n412\t31.4\n413\t25.6\n414\t\n415\t37.8\n416\t36.5\n417\t33.3\n418\t\n419\t52.6\n420\t55.8\n421\t50.6\n422\t\n423\tQwen3\n424\t42.9 48.3 64.3\n425\tQwen2.5 39.8 43.9 62.1\n426\tPhi-4\n427\t43.6 75.2 62.1\n428\t\n429\t46.7\n430\t41.4\n431\t36.7\n432\t\n433\t55.8\n434\t53.0\n435\t46.4\n436\t\n437\t63.3\n438\t66.8\n439\t78.1\n440\t\n441\t34.4 43.9 49.6\n442\t\n443\t35.1\n444\t\n445\t41.0\n446\t\n447\t60.0\n448\t\n449\tDataset\n450\t\n451\tCorpus\n452\tAgreement\n453\t\n454\tGenerated\n455\tAgreement\n456\t\n457\tHuman\n458\tFleiss’ κ\n459\t\n460\tCReST\n461\t\n462\tMapTask\n463\tFindTask\n464\tCReST\n465\t\n466\t78.6%\n467\t81.6%\n468\t77.2%\n469\t\n470\t76.4%\n471\t67.8%\n472\t75.5%\n473\t\n474\t0.659\n475\t0.615\n476\t0.718\n477\t\n478\tAvg.\n479\t\n480\tOverall\n481\t\n482\t78.5%\n483\t\n484\t73.9%\n485\t\n486\t0.682\n487\t\n488\tTable 4: Oracle-target move-realization accuracy (%). All\n489\tmethods receive the gold target move.\n490\t\n491\tTable 2: Move-classifier validation against corpus labels and\n492\tmajority judgments from three blinded annotators; Fleiss’ κ\n493\tmeasures inter-annotator agreement.\n494\t\n495\tResults\n496\tWe evaluate the proposed framework at three levels: nextmove selection, oracle-target realization, and end-to-end conversational control. Experiments span Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu\n497\t\n498\tWe evaluate oracle realization by providing every method\n499\twith the dialogue context and gold follower move. Baselines include direct prompting (Base), few-shot candidate\n500\tselection (PBL) (Ramirez et al. 2023), instruction-derived\n501\tsteering (SI) (Stolfo et al. 2025), classifier-guided decoding\n502\t(FUDGE) (Yang and Klein 2021), and reward-guided search\n503\t(DeAL) (Huang et al. 2025). Latent-IM applies move directions learned exclusively from MapTask and transferred\n504\t\n505\t\fDataset\n506\t\n507\tModel\n508\t\n509\tBaseline\n510\t\n511\tPrompt Control\n512\t\n513\tPAS\n514\t\n515\tSFT\n516\t\n517\tLatent-IM\n518\t\n519\tAcc.\n520\t\n521\tBLEU\n522\t\n523\tAcc.\n524\t\n525\tBLEU\n526\t\n527\tAcc.\n528\t\n529\tBLEU\n530\t\n531\tAcc.\n532\t\n533\tBLEU\n534\t\n535\tAcc.\n536\t\n537\tBLEU\n538\t\n539\tMapTask\n540\t\n541\tQwen3\n542\t28.98\n543\tQwen2.5 30.87\n544\tPhi-4\n545\t40.59\n546\t\n547\t8.38\n548\t11.29\n549\t19.68\n550\t\n551\t19.39\n552\t24.24\n553\t33.13\n554\t\n555\t16.66\n556\t10.24\n557\t14.56\n558\t\n559\t25.25\n560\t29.90\n561\t34.75\n562\t\n563\t8.72\n564\t10.50\n565\t14.58\n566\t\n567\t45.05\n568\t46.46\n569\t46.26\n570\t\n571\t26.44\n572\t26.29\n573\t25.66\n574\t\n575\t30.35\n576\t41.33\n577\t46.47\n578\t\n579\t10.12\n580\t20.52\n581\t20.33\n582\t\n583\tFindTask\n584\t\n585\tQwen3\n586\t29.49\n587\tQwen2.5 23.72\n588\tPhi-4\n589\t29.49\n590\t\n591\t8.01\n592\t12.24\n593\t6.96\n594\t\n595\t39.10\n596\t34.62\n597\t28.95\n598\t\n599\t16.43\n600\t15.07\n601\t10.68\n602\t\n603\t27.56\n604\t21.79\n605\t34.62\n606\t\n607\t8.10\n608\t11.83\n609\t6.63\n610\t\n611\t44.87\n612\t25.00\n613\t45.51\n614\t\n615\t26.56\n616\t17.30\n617\t31.69\n618\t\n619\t49.36\n620\t48.72\n621\t48.08\n622\t\n623\t10.61\n624\t16.34\n625\t6.92\n626\t\n627\tCReST\n628\t\n629\tQwen3\n630\t42.95\n631\tQwen2.5 39.81\n632\tPhi-4\n633\t43.57\n634\t\n635\t9.49\n636\t12.62\n637\t17.59\n638\t\n639\t12.54\n640\t21.00\n641\t35.42\n642\t\n643\t19.29\n644\t10.81\n645\t10.60\n646\t\n647\t45.77\n648\t42.01\n649\t46.08\n650\t\n651\t10.17\n652\t12.94\n653\t14.22\n654\t\n655\t56.11\n656\t55.49\n657\t54.86\n658\t\n659\t23.79\n660\t23.87\n661\t24.06\n662\t\n663\t52.04\n664\t47.02\n665\t58.62\n666\t\n667\t10.25\n668\t16.61\n669\t18.14\n670\t\n671\tTable 5: End-to-end move-control results across dialogue datasets and multimodal language models. Acc. is the percentage of\n672\tgenerated responses whose predicted conversational move matches the held-out human move. BLEU measures lexical overlap\n673\twith the held-out human response.\n674\t\n675\tunchanged to all evaluation sets. It achieves the highest mean\n676\taccuracy (60.0%) and leads in six of nine settings.\n677\t\n678\tEnd-to-End Move Control\n679\tFinally, we evaluate end-to-end response generation, where\n680\tall methods receive the same held-out dialogue context\n681\tand no gold move labels at inference. Table 5 compares\n682\ttwo explicit selection–realization pipelines with three directgeneration baselines. Prompt Control first predicts a conversational move using an LLM selector and then includes\n683\tthat prediction in a definitional prompt. Latent-IM instead\n684\tuses the streaming activation controller to select the next\n685\tmove and realizes it through fixed move-specific steering directions. Base, PAS, and SFT do not explicitly predict or\n686\tcondition on move labels: Base generates directly from the\n687\tdialogue context, PAS applies its learned intervention directly during generation, and SFT separately fine-tunes each\n688\tbackbone with LoRA (Hu et al. 2022) on context–response\n689\tpairs to imitate the human response.\n690\tLatent-IM achieves the highest mean end-to-end move\n691\taccuracy at 46.9%, slightly exceeding SFT at 46.6% and outperforming Base (34.4%), PAS (34.2%), and Prompt Control (27.6%). It obtains the highest accuracy in the majority of the dataset–model settings. SFT remains stronger for\n692\tQwen3 and Qwen2.5 on MapTask and CReST and produces\n693\thigher BLEU (Papineni et al. 2002) scores overall, consistent\n694\twith direct supervised imitation of the reference response. In\n695\tcontrast, Latent-IM often realizes the appropriate conversational move using lexically different wording. These results show that explicitly recovering and controlling an intermediate conversational action can match direct supervised\n696\tfine-tuning while retaining a modular, label-free inference\n697\tpipeline.\n698\t\n699\tTurn-Boundary Subspace\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "568874b8-8b57-4156-a4b8-cd5669c4cba7", "uuid": "ae4f089d-5292-450b-93e2-1e88c1f0f3d8", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T08:39:16.904Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/latent_im.txt", "content": "L\not−1 . (2)\n\nThe length output is monotonically calibrated to a turnboundary steering strength and, like all steering decisions,\nacts through the move-level gating rule below; move-steering\nstrengths are per-move constants selected on training data.\nRealization-aware gating. Based on training-set steerability, move steering is withheld for reply, which the unsteered\nbackbone realizes more reliably, while turn-boundary steering is applied only to naturally terse moves (acknowledge,\nquery, and reply). All gates depend on the controller prediction rather than gold labels.\nTraining and inference. With the backbone frozen, we\ntrain the controller using next-move cross-entropy and\nsmooth-L1 log-length loss. All components use the same\n\ndialogue-level split, excluding held-out test dialogues. At inference, the controller maintains a persistent streaming state\nacross turns.\n\nConversational-Move Subspaces for Realization\nFor each follower turn t in the training dialogues, with gold\nmove yt ∈ M, we extract the residual-stream change at\nresponse onset. Let hℓt (s) ∈ Rd denote the activation at layer\nℓ and token position s, and let st,1 denote the position of the\nfirst token of response ut . We define\n∆ℓt = hℓt (st,1 ) − hℓt (st,1 − 1),\n\n(3)\n\nwhich captures the transition from dialogue context to response generation. For each backbone, move directions are\nestimated exclusively from its activations on the MapTask\ntraining split and then applied, without re-estimation, to the\nsame backbone on the held-out MapTask test split, FindTask,\nand CReST.\nAt each layer, we standardize the onset differences\n{∆ℓt }t∈Itrain , project them into a k-dimensional PCA space,\nand estimate a one-versus-rest Fisher direction for each move\nusing Ledoit–Wolf shrinkage (Ledoit and Wolf 2004; Fisher\nℓ\nℓ\n1936). Let νm\nand ν¬m\ndenote the means of move m and\nb ℓw,m denote the\nall remaining moves in PCA space, and let Σ\n\n\fshrinkage estimate of their pooled within-class covariance.\nThe discriminative direction is\n\u0010\n\u0011−1\n\u0001\nℓ\nℓ\nℓ\nb ℓw,m\nwm\n∝ Σ\nνm\n− ν¬m\n.\n(4)\n\net al. 2025a), Qwen3-Omni (Xu et al. 2025b), and Phi-4Multimodal (Abouelenin et al. 2025).\n\nSelection: Predicting the Next Move\n\nℓ\nWe map wm\nback to residual-stream coordinates, undo\n\nthe feature scaling, and normalize the result to obtain the\nℓ\nmove direction vm\n. The inverse covariance suppresses dimensions with high within-class variation, yielding a more\ndiscriminative direction than a simple difference of means.\nFor target move m, we inject its direction at the selected\nlayer ℓ⋆m at every generated token position s:\n⋆\n\nℓ⋆\n\n⋆\n\nhℓm (s) ← hℓm (s) + αcℓ⋆m vmm .\n(5)\nwhere cℓm is the median residual-stream norm at that layer\nand α is a dimensionless steering coefficient. This normrelative scaling improves comparability across layers and\nmodel backbones.\n\nAutomatic Move Classifier\nWe use Qwen2.5-72B (Yang et al. 2025) as a prompted\nfew-shot classifier over five move types, using the same\nprompt and decoding procedure across datasets. Classification is context-dependent: we first label the preceding\ngiver turn, then classify the follower response conditioned\non that label, using the gold giver label when available. Decoding is restricted to one option token, whose normalized\nlog-probabilities define P (move | ·). On human corpus responses, the classifier recovers the gold move with 77.2%–\n81.6% accuracy (Table 2). For generation evaluation, we\nwithhold the human response, generate a replacement, and\ncompare its classifier-assigned move with the original human\ngold label. We further validate it on 500 generated responses,\nbalanced across target moves, datasets, and backbone models. Three blinded annotators label each response from its\ndialogue context. The classifier agrees with their majority label on 73.9% of examples, while human agreement is Fleiss’\nκ = 0.682. Across the seven generation methods, classifierbased and human-majority move accuracies are strongly correlated (Spearman ρ = 0.96) and induce nearly the same\nranking, so the automatic metric does not systematically favor any particular method.\n\nSelector\n\nMapTask\n\nFindTask\n\nCReST\n\nAvg.\n\nMajority baseline\nTF-IDF + logistic (last 5 turns)\nGPT-4o (few-shot)\nQwen2.5-72B (few-shot)\nMarkov-1 (gold prev. move)\nTranscript-embed. probe\nTranscript-embed. SSM\nActivation probe\n\n0.47\n0.54\n0.40\n0.38\n0.65\n0.59\n0.58\n0.64\n\n0.41\n0.46\n0.32\n0.38\n0.42\n0.53\n0.50\n0.50\n\n0.37\n0.44\n0.53\n0.41\n0.56\n0.58\n0.60\n0.61\n\n0.42\n0.48\n0.42\n0.39\n0.54\n0.57\n0.56\n0.58\n\nController (ours, streaming)\n\n0.62\n\n0.58\n\n0.61\n\n0.60\n\nTable 3: Next-move selection accuracy. Markov-1 receives\nthe gold preceding move; the activation probe and the controller read the residual stream at the selected controller layer\nℓ⋆ctrl .\nThe activation controller outperforms all transcript-based selectors across datasets. On MapTask it reaches 0.62, close to\nthe gold-history Markov baseline at 0.65, while on FindTask\nand CReST it surpasses that baseline. The non-recurrent activation probe performs similarly on MapTask and CReST,\nsuggesting that the backbone already integrates the relevant\ndialogue history. On FindTask, the stateful controller improves over the probe by 0.08, indicating that explicit crossturn state is useful for shorter episodes.\n\nRealization: Generating a Target Move\nDataset\n\nModel\n\nBase\n\nSI\n\nPBL FUDGE DeAL Ours\n\nQwen3\n29.0 30.1 43.2\nMapTask Qwen2.5 30.9 38.8 42.4\nPhi-4\n40.6 63.2 56.6\n\n29.9\n30.7\n46.7\n\n34.9\n39.8\n31.1\n\n41.0\n66.8\n64.8\n\nQwen3\n29.5 34.6 46.2\nFindTask Qwen2.5 23.7 25.6 34.0\nPhi-4\n29.5 35.3 35.9\n\n26.9\n31.4\n25.6\n\n37.8\n36.5\n33.3\n\n52.6\n55.8\n50.6\n\nQwen3\n42.9 48.3 64.3\nQwen2.5 39.8 43.9 62.1\nPhi-4\n43.6 75.2 62.1\n\n46.7\n41.4\n36.7\n\n55.8\n53.0\n46.4\n\n63.3\n66.8\n78.1\n\n34.4 43.9 49.6\n\n35.1\n\n41.0\n\n60.0\n\nDataset\n\nCorpus\nAgreement\n\nGenerated\nAgreement\n\nHuman\nFleiss’ κ\n\nCReST\n\nMapTask\nFindTask\nCReST\n\n78.6%\n81.6%\n77.2%\n\n76.4%\n67.8%\n75.5%\n\n0.659\n0.615\n0.718\n\nAvg.\n\nOverall\n\n78.5%\n\n73.9%\n\n0.682\n\nTable 4: Oracle-target move-realization accuracy (%). All\nmethods receive the gold target move.\n\nTable 2: Move-classifier validation against corpus labels and\nmajority judgments from three blinded annotators; Fleiss’ κ\nmeasures inter-annotator agreement.\n\nResults\nWe evaluate the proposed framework at three levels: nextmove selection, oracle-target realization, and end-to-end conversational control. Experiments span Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu\n\nWe evaluate oracle realization by providing every method\nwith the dialogue context and gold follower move. Baselines include direct prompting (Base), few-shot candidate\nselection (PBL) (Ramirez et al. 2023), instruction-derived\nsteering (SI) (Stolfo et al. 2025), classifier-guided decoding\n(FUDGE) (Yang and Klein 2021), and reward-guided search\n(DeAL) (Huang et al. 2025). Latent-IM applies move directions learned exclusively from MapTask and transferred\n\n\fDataset\n\nModel\n\nBaseline\n\nPrompt Control\n\nPAS\n\nSFT\n\nLatent-IM\n\nAcc.\n\nBLEU\n\nAcc.\n\nBLEU\n\nAcc.\n\nBLEU\n\nAcc.\n\nBLEU\n\nAcc.\n\nBLEU\n\nMapTask\n\nQwen3\n28.98\nQwen2.5 30.87\nPhi-4\n40.59\n\n8.38\n11.29\n19.68\n\n19.39\n24.24\n33.13\n\n16.66\n10.24\n14.56\n\n25.25\n29.90\n34.75\n\n8.72\n10.50\n14.58\n\n45.05\n46.46\n46.26\n\n26.44\n26.29\n25.66\n\n30.35\n41.33\n46.47\n\n10.12\n20.52\n20.33\n\nFindTask\n\nQwen3\n29.49\nQwen2.5 23.72\nPhi-4\n29.49\n\n8.01\n12.24\n6.96\n\n39.10\n34.62\n28.95\n\n16.43\n15.07\n10.68\n\n27.56\n21.79\n34.62\n\n8.10\n11.83\n6.63\n\n44.87\n25.00\n45.51\n\n26.56\n17.30\n31.69\n\n49.36\n48.72\n48.08\n\n10.61\n16.34\n6.92\n\nCReST\n\nQwen3\n42.95\nQwen2.5 39.81\nPhi-4\n43.57\n\n9.49\n12.62\n17.59\n\n12.54\n21.00\n35.42\n\n19.29\n10.81\n10.60\n\n45.77\n42.01\n46.08\n\n10.17\n12.94\n14.22\n\n56.11\n55.49\n54.86\n\n23.79\n23.87\n24.06\n\n52.04\n47.02\n58.62\n\n10.25\n16.61\n18.14\n\nTable 5: End-to-end move-control results across dialogue datasets and multimodal language models. Acc. is the percentage of\ngenerated responses whose predicted conversational move matches the held-out human move. BLEU measures lexical overlap\nwith the held-out human response.\n\nunchanged to all evaluation sets. It achieves the highest mean\naccuracy (60.0%) and leads in six of nine settings.\n\nEnd-to-End Move Control\nFinally, we evaluate end-to-end response generation, where\nall methods receive the same held-out dialogue context\nand no gold move labels at inference. Table 5 compares\ntwo explicit selection–realization pipelines with three directgeneration baselines. Prompt Control first predicts a conversational move using an LLM selector and then includes\nthat prediction in a definitional prompt. Latent-IM instead\nuses the streaming activation controller to select the next\nmove and realizes it through fixed move-specific steering directions. Base, PAS, and SFT do not explicitly predict or\ncondition on move labels: Base generates directly from the\ndialogue context, PAS applies its learned intervention directly during generation, and SFT separately fine-tunes each\nbackbone with LoRA (Hu et al. 2022) on context–response\npairs to imitate the human response.\nLatent-IM achieves the highest mean end-to-end move\naccuracy at 46.9%, slightly exceeding SFT at 46.6% and outperforming Base (34.4%), PAS (34.2%), and Prompt Control (27.6%). It obtains the highest accuracy in the majority of the dataset–model settings. SFT remains stronger for\nQwen3 and Qwen2.5 on MapTask and CReST and produces\nhigher BLEU (Papineni et al. 2002) scores overall, consistent\nwith direct supervised imitation of the reference response. In\ncontrast, Latent-IM often realizes the appropriate conversational move using lexically different wording. These results show that explicitly recovering and controlling an intermediate conversational action can match direct supervised\nfine-tuning while retaining a modular, label-free inference\npipeline.\n\nTurn-Boundary Subspace", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 200, "totalLines": 1885}}}
