{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b17d85131b6a65d6", "type": "tool_result", "content": "700\tPredicting whether a speaker will continue or yield the\n701\tfloor is a longstanding problem in spoken dialogue modeling (Skantze 2017; Ekstedt and Skantze 2020; Lin et al.\n702\t2025). We identify a turn-boundary direction representing\n703\twhether the current response should continue or terminate,\n704\tcomplementing acoustic pauses that may occur within a turn\n705\t(Lala et al. 2017). We derive supervision from the speaker\n706\tsequence of the MapTask training split. For each turn t < T ,\n707\twe assign\n708\tbt = 1[rt+1 ̸= rt ] ,\n709\t(6)\n710\twhere bt = 1 denotes yielding the floor and bt = 0 denotes\n711\tcontinuing. At each layer, we mean-pool the residual stream\n712\tover the turn’s token span. A layer sweep selects layer ℓ⋆eot ,\n713\tand the unit-norm turn-boundary direction is estimated by\n714\tthe difference between the yield and hold class means:\n715\tveot =\n716\t\n717\tµyield − µhold\n718\t.\n719\t∥µyield − µhold ∥2\n720\t\n721\t(7)\n722\t\n723\tLike the move directions, veot is learned only from MapTask\n724\ttraining dialogues and transferred without re-estimation to all\n725\tevaluation sets. The direction is predictive: on held-out follower turns it detects upcoming boundaries at 0.95 AUROC\n726\tfrom content alone, versus 0.932 for a pause-only detector,\n727\twhich is also post hoc since it can only score a boundary\n728\tafter the silence has elapsed. An incremental spoken system\n729\tneeds the former to yield the floor without a gap. During generation, we inject the direction using the same norm-relative\n730\trule as move steering:\n731\t⋆\n732\t\n733\t⋆\n734\t\n735\thℓeot (s) ← hℓeot (s) + βcℓ⋆eot veot .\n736\t\n737\t(8)\n738\t\n739\tPositive β encourages termination, while negative β prolongs\n740\tthe response. Figure 2 sweeps β over [−1, 1] on 250 held-out\n741\ttest contexts with only the turn-boundary direction injected:\n742\tmean response length varies monotonically from 71.3 words\n743\t\n744\t\fFigure 2: Steering along the turn-boundary direction monotonically controls response length (mean ±95% CI).\n745\tat β= − 1 through the unsteered 24.9 down to 10.4 at β=1,\n746\twith decoding remaining fluent throughout, giving graded,\n747\tsample-free control over when the model yields the turn.\n748\t\n749\tAnalysis\n750\tController\n751\tState is read, not recomputed. Table 3 separates what\n752\tcarries the dialogue state from what reads it. The transcriptembed rows see the same token sequence as the controller but\n753\tthrough the backbone’s embedding table alone; with matched\n754\treaders, replacing contextual activations with these lexical\n755\tfeatures costs three to five points on MapTask and CReST,\n756\tand on FindTask the gap is closed only by a reader that\n757\tcarries explicit cross-turn state. The state is computed by the\n758\tfrozen backbone and merely read out. Reading it is also more\n759\treliable than asking for it: prompting GPT-4o or Qwen2.572B to name the next move falls 0.08–0.26 below the 0.5M\n760\tcontroller. Finally, the reader’s architecture barely matters:\n761\tMLP, GRU, LSTM, and SSM variants all reach ≈ 0.60 on\n762\tMapTask, which is what one expects if the state is linearly\n763\tavailable in the residual stream at each turn. We adopt the\n764\tselective SSM because it formalizes the per-turn state update\n765\twith a constant-time streaming step, not because recurrence\n766\twins on accuracy; the exception is FindTask’s short episodes,\n767\twhere stateless readers drop to ≈ 0.50 and carried state is\n768\twhat remains.\n769\tSelection is not enough. Higher selection accuracy does\n770\tnot always translate into better end-to-end control, because\n771\tper-move predictability and steerability tend to trade off: a\n772\tmove the controller predicts confidently is not necessarily\n773\tone that steering can realize. The clearest case is reply:\n774\tafter a giver question the follower almost always replies, so a\n775\tgiver-move prior sharply improves reply selection (precision\n776\t0.53 → 0.64), yet this yields no end-to-end gain, because\n777\tthe reply steering direction surface-overlaps acknowledge\n778\tand does not land (controllability 0.14 vs. 0.99 for query).\n779\tThis motivates realization-aware gating: apply the actionsubspace steering only where it lands.\n780\t\n781\tSelection under ambiguity. Selection accuracy must be\n782\tinterpreted against how consistent humans are in the same\n783\tsituation. Even near-identical giver turns license different follower moves: in our held-out set, after the identical giver turn\n784\t“do you see what I mean” one follower replies (“yeah”) while\n785\tanother queries (“so do I go up straight first?”); after nearidentical instructions (“. . . centimetres from the top/bottom\n786\tof the page”) one follower checks (“the bottom of the page\n787\tyou mean”) while another acknowledges (“mm right”). We\n788\tfind 55 such similar-giver/different-follower pairs in the test\n789\tdialogues alone. Aggregating by the preceding giver move,\n790\tthe gold follower move is concentrated after questions (after\n791\tquery-yn, 75% of humans reply) but genuinely open after\n792\tinstructions and acknowledgements (mode probability 0.30–\n793\t0.51).\n794\tThe controller inherits exactly this structure. Splitting\n795\theld-out turns by human consistency: on clear contexts\n796\t(mode ≥ 0.6) it reaches 0.61 against a human-consistency\n797\tceiling of 0.68 (89%); on open contexts it reaches 0.46\n798\tagainst a ceiling of 0.49 (95%). Per giver-move type, controller accuracy tracks the human mode probability nearly\n799\tone-to-one (e.g., query-yn: 0.758 vs. 0.75), and its predictive entropy correlates with the true follower-move entropy\n800\tat r = 0.95. Its confusions flow into acknowledge—the\n801\tmodal move—which is the Bayes-optimal guess under ambiguity rather than a failure mode. The ceiling is also modalityinvariant: adding the follower’s map as text (0.526) or as an\n802\timage through the vision pathway (0.521) leaves selection at\n803\tthe no-map level (0.524). Together these results indicate the\n804\tremaining selection gap is irreducible follower choice, not\n805\tmissing features; moving past it requires scoring moves by\n806\ttask outcome rather than matching a single human reference,\n807\twhich we leave to future work.\n808\tRealization-aware gating. The remedy is a gating rule on\n809\tthe action subspace, not a new controller: for each predicted\n810\tmove we decide whether to inject the move direction and\n811\twhether to inject the turn-length direction, both read from the\n812\tcontroller’s own prediction (no gold labels). The rule follows\n813\tmeasured per-move steerability: steer where the controllability matrix is strong (check, query, explain), defer where\n814\tit is weak (reply). Deferring reply raises end-to-end realization from 0.67 to 0.75 at no selection cost; length-aware\n815\tcontrol adds a further gain to 0.78. Length is handled as move\n816\tpolicy rather than per-turn regression: the controller maps its\n817\tselected move to a turn-boundary coefficient, pulling the naturally terse moves (acknowledge, query, reply) from ∼ 25\n818\twords toward the human median of 2–3. For content-bearing\n819\tmoves (check, explain) the intervention is withheld: the\n820\tinjection needed to shorten them overwhelms the move direction and collapses realization (explain 0.76 → 0.28). A\n821\tcontroller that models the geometry of its own steering outperforms one that only predicts moves accurately.\n822\t\n823\tSteering\n824\tTo test whether steering directions are move-specific, we apply each move vector to the same held-out contexts and classify the resulting generations. The resulting controllability\n825\tmatrix shows whether each intervention selectively increases\n826\t\n827\t\fAbl.\n828\tDefault\n829\t\n830\tAcc.\n831\t\n832\t∆\n833\t\n834\tBLEU\n835\t\n836\tα , 0.65\n837\t\n838\t–\n839\t\n840\t0.18\n841\t\n842\tSetting\n843\tAudio+text,\n844\tℓ⋆ , residual\n845\t\n846\t⋆\n847\t\n848\tModality Text only\n849\t\n850\tFigure 3: Controllability matrix for move steering (map-task,\n851\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B). Each row applies one steering direction\n852\tto the same held-out contexts; columns give the move assigned to the generation by the classifier\n853\t\n854\tits corresponding conversational move rather than merely\n855\tperturbing generation. Figure 3 shows that each steering direction selectively increases its intended move, with a mean\n856\tdiagonal controllability of 0.66 and limited off-diagonal leakage. Relative to the unsteered baseline, steering raises query\n857\tfrom 0.05 to 0.96 and explain from 0.07 to 0.70, while acknowledge and check rise to 0.65 and 0.70. These results\n858\tindicate that the interventions causally target move-specific\n859\tbehavior rather than generically perturbing generation.\n860\tTable 6 ablates the main design choices in the steering\n861\tintervention using oracle target moves; each row varies one\n862\tcomponent while holding the others at the default configuration. Steering raises target-move accuracy from 0.33 to\n863\t0.65, a 0.31-point gain. The modality comparison shows that\n864\tspoken information contributes signal beyond the transcript\n865\talone: audio+text outperforms text-only (0.65 vs. 0.58). The\n866\tstrength sweep exposes a clear controllability tradeoff: accuracy improves up to a moderate strength (α ≈ 1.0, 0.64) but\n867\tfalls to 0.55 at α = 2.0. The layer ablation shows that earlylayer injection is substantially weaker (0.25) than middle- or\n868\tlate-layer injection (0.48/0.47), while no fixed layer matches\n869\tthe per-move tuned default (0.65). Finally, control is largely\n870\tinsensitive to injection site: residual-stream, attention, and\n871\tMLP interventions obtain 0.65, 0.65, and 0.64, respectively.\n872\tOverall, steering depends on multimodal context, moderate\n873\tintervention strength, and layer choice, while remaining robust to injection location.\n874\t\n875\tFuture Work: Beyond Human-Move Imitation\n876\tOur controller imitates the move a human is likely to make,\n877\tbut the best task-oriented move may differ from the human choice. Because our steering mechanism can realize\n878\tany move in the taxonomy, a future goal-conditioned controller could select moves according to predicted task utility\n879\t\n880\t0.58 −0.07\n881\t\n882\t0.19\n883\t\n884\tStrength\n885\tStrength\n886\tStrength\n887\tStrength\n888\t\n889\tα = 0.0\n890\tα = 0.5\n891\tα = 1.0\n892\tα = 2.0\n893\t\n894\t0.33\n895\t0.53\n896\t0.64\n897\t0.55\n898\t\n899\t−0.31\n900\t−0.12\n901\t−0.01\n902\t−0.10\n903\t\n904\t0.11\n905\t0.14\n906\t0.17\n907\t0.19\n908\t\n909\tLayer\n910\tLayer\n911\tLayer\n912\t\n913\tEarly\n914\tMiddle\n915\tLate\n916\t\n917\t0.25 −0.40\n918\t0.48 −0.17\n919\t0.47 −0.18\n920\t\n921\t0.10\n922\t0.19\n923\t0.09\n924\t\n925\tSite\n926\tSite\n927\t\n928\tAttention\n929\tMLP\n930\t\n931\t0.65 0.00\n932\t0.64 −0.01\n933\t\n934\t0.18\n935\t0.18\n936\t\n937\tTable 6: Steering ablations on held-out MapTask contexts\n938\tusing Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and oracle target moves. Each row\n939\tvaries one component of the default configuration while\n940\tholding the others fixed. ∆ denotes the change in target-move\n941\taccuracy relative to the default; BLEU measures lexical overlap with the human response.\n942\t\n943\trather than imitation likelihood.\n944\tMapTask: an acknowledgment hides a map mismatch\n945\tGiver: “Walk north until you reach the Roman baths.” [instruct]\n946\tFollower: “Uh-huh.” [acknowledge]\n947\tGiver: “Then walk right around them.” [instruct]\n948\tFollower: “Roman baths? I don’t have any Roman baths.”\n949\t[query]\n950\tThe follower initially acknowledges an instruction whose referent is absent from their map. Although acknowledgment\n951\tmatches the observed human move, an earlier check or query,\n952\tsuch as “Roman baths? I do not have those on my map,” would\n953\treveal the mismatch before it propagates into later instructions.\n954\t\n955\tConclusion\n956\tWe introduced Latent-IM, which separates conversationalmove selection from realization within a frozen speech LLM.\n957\tA streaming controller reads residual-stream activations to\n958\tselect the next move, reaching 0.60 average accuracy, while\n959\tactivation steering improves oracle realization by 10.4 percentage points over the strongest baseline. A complementary turn-boundary direction reduces response length by 9.1\n960\twords, and the full system improves end-to-end move accuracy by 12.5 points over the unsteered backbone while\n961\tperforming comparably to fine-tuning. These results provide\n962\tevidence that frozen speech LLM activations can serve as\n963\tan observable and causally controllable interface for local\n964\tinteraction management. Future work can replace imitationbased selection with goal-conditioned policies that choose\n965\tmoves according to predicted task utility.\n966\t\n967\t\fReferences\n968\tAbouelenin, A.; Ashfaq, A.; Atkinson, A.; Awadalla, H.;\n969\tBach, N.; Bao, J.; Benhaim, A.; Cai, M.; Chaudhary, V.;\n970\tChen, C.; et al. 2025. Phi-4-mini technical report: Compact\n971\tyet powerful multimodal language models via mixture-ofloras. arXiv preprint arXiv:2503.01743.\n972\tAnderson, A. H.; Bader, M.; Bard, E. G.; Boyle, E.; Doherty,\n973\tG.; Garrod, S.; Isard, S.; Kowtko, J.; McAllister, J.; Miller,\n974\tJ.; et al. 1991. The HCRC map task corpus. Language and\n975\tspeech, 34(4): 351–366.\n976\tAvsian, A.; and Heck, L. 2026. SNEAK: Evaluating Strategic\n977\tCommunication and Information Leakage in Large Language\n978\tModels. arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.29846.\n979\tAvsian, A.; Richardson, C.; Sundar, A.; and Heck, L. 2026.\n980\tFLEx: Language Modeling with Few-shot Language Explanations. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.04157.\n981\tChang, C.-K.; Chang, K.-W.; Liu, A. H.; and Glass, J.\n982\t2026. Overcoming State Inertia in Full-Duplex Spoken\n983\tLanguage Models via Activation Steering. arXiv preprint\n984\tarXiv:2606.11386.\n985\tChen, L.; and Di Eugenio, B. 2013. Multimodality and Dialogue Act Classification in the RoboHelper Project. In Eskenazi, M.; Strube, M.; Di Eugenio, B.; and Williams, J. D.,\n986\teds., Proceedings of the SIGDIAL 2013 Conference, 183–\n987\t192. Metz, France: Association for Computational Linguistics.\n988\tClark, H. H.; and Brennan, S. E. 1991. Grounding in communication.\n989\tCui, S.; and Chen, Z. 2026. Painless Activation Steering: An\n990\tAutomated, Lightweight Approach for Post-Training Large\n991\tLanguage Models. arXiv:2509.22739.\n992\tDokme, A.; and Heck, L. 2026. Selective State-Space Adaptation and Retrieval for Language Model Reasoning. arXiv\n993\tpreprint arXiv:2607.19326.\n994\tDokme, A.; Reichman, B.; and Heck, L. 2026. TEMPER:\n995\tTesting Emotional Perturbation in Quantitative Reasoning.\n996\tIn Proceedings of the Conference on Language Modeling\n997\t(COLM).\n998\tEberhard, K.; Nicholson, H.; Kübler, S.; Gundersen, S.; and\n999\tScheutz, M. 2010. The Indiana “Cooperative Remote Search\n1000\tTask” (CReST) Corpus. In Calzolari, N.; Choukri, K.; Maegaard, B.; Mariani, J.; Odijk, J.; Piperidis, S.; Rosner, M.;\n1001\tand Tapias, D., eds., Proceedings of the Seventh International Conference on Language Resources and Evaluation\n1002\t(LREC’10). Valletta, Malta: European Language Resources\n1003\tAssociation (ELRA).\n1004\tEkstedt, E.; and Skantze, G. 2020. TurnGPT: a transformerbased language model for predicting turn-taking in spoken\n1005\tdialog. In Findings of the Association for Computational\n1006\tLinguistics: EMNLP 2020, 2981–2990.\n1007\tFisher, R. A. 1936. The use of multiple measurements in\n1008\ttaxonomic problems. Annals of eugenics, 7(2): 179–188.\n1009\tGu, A.; and Dao, T. 2023. Mamb