{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a474b27038b4cde2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1200\t\n1201\tThe preceding turn was labeled: {PRECEDING_MOVE}\n1202\t\n1203\tAutomatic Move Classifier\n1204\t\n1205\tFinal turn to classify:\n1206\t“{MODEL_RESPONSE}”\n1207\t\n1208\tThe automatic evaluator classifies the generated follower turn\n1209\tinto one of five moves:\n1210\t\n1211\tAnswer with exactly one word from: acknowledge,\n1212\tcheck, explain, query, or reply.\n1213\t\n1214\tM = {acknowledge, check, explain, query, reply}.\n1215\t\n1216\tAnswer:\n1217\t\n1218\tDecoding is constrained to these five label tokens. To remove\n1219\tdataset-specific cues, speaker prefixes are normalized to A\n1220\tand B. The classifier receives the immediately preceding\n1221\tturn, its move label when available, and the follower response\n1222\tbeing evaluated.\n1223\tSystem:\n1224\tTwo people are doing a task together by talking. Read the\n1225\trecent exchange and classify the FINAL turn by what it\n1226\tis doing in the conversation. Choose exactly one:\n1227\tacknowledge – only signals that the speaker heard or\n1228\tregistered the other; adds no new information and asks\n1229\tnothing (“okay,” “mm-hmm,” “right,” “uh-huh,” “I see”).\n1230\tcheck – confirms something already said or inferred by\n1231\trestating, paraphrasing, or pointing to it (“so I go left?”,\n1232\t“the blue one?”, “this one?”).\n1233\t\n1234\tBaselines\n1235\tPrompt-Based Learning, Steering-Instruct, and FUDGE receive the target move in the oracle-realization setting. SFT\n1236\tis an end-to-end baseline and receives no move label during\n1237\ttraining or inference.\n1238\tPrompt-Based Learning (PBL). PBL (Ramirez et al.\n1239\t2023) prepends N = 10 in-context demonstrations sampled\n1240\tfrom the training split using seed 42. Each demonstration\n1241\tcontains at most the three most recent context turns. Demonstrations are sampled at run time rather than drawn from a\n1242\tsingle fixed set.\n1243\tThe default target-shaped template is:\n1244\tHere is a dialogue context:\n1245\t{CONTEXT}\n1246\t\n1247\t\fThe follower’s next response,\n1248\twritten as a ‘{MOVE}’ dialogue act:\n1249\t{RESPONSE}\n1250\tAt test time, the same template is used but ends immediately after dialogue act:. The definitional variant prepends the target move’s definition. The fewshot\n1251\tvariant instead uses:\n1252\tContext: {CONTEXT}\n1253\tResponse ({MOVE}): {RESPONSE}\n1254\tFor each example, the model samples K = 10 candidates\n1255\tusing temperature 0.7, top-p = 1.0, and at most 60 new\n1256\ttokens. Among candidates that the move classifier assigns to\n1257\tthe target move, PBL selects the one with the highest mean\n1258\ttoken log-probability. If no candidate is assigned to the target\n1259\tmove, it selects\n1260\tŜ = arg max Pcls (m | ct , S) PG (S | ct ).\n1261\tS\n1262\t\n1263\tSteering-Instruct. Steering-Instruct (Stolfo et al. 2025)\n1264\tcontrasts a bare dialogue context against three moveconditioned prompt variants:\n1265\t\n1266\twhere λ = 1. The highest-scoring token is selected greedily,\n1267\twithout sampling or beam search.\n1268\tSupervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). SFT is an end-to-end\n1269\tbaseline that fine-tunes the backbone on context–response\n1270\tpairs,\n1271\tct −→ ut ,\n1272\twithout providing a move label during training or inference\n1273\t(Ouyang et al. 2022).\n1274\tWe apply LoRA with rank r = 16, scaling parameter\n1275\t32, dropout 0.05, and all linear layers as target modules\n1276\t(Hu et al. 2022). Training is text-only and uses up to 1,000\n1277\ttraining pairs, with loss computed only over response tokens.\n1278\tWe use AdamW with learning rate 2 × 10−4 , three epochs,\n1279\tper-device batch size 1, gradient accumulation over 16 steps,\n1280\tBF16 precision, and seed 42. The final-epoch weights are\n1281\tretained without validation-based checkpoint selection. For\n1282\tQwen2.5-Omni, approximately 65M of 9.0B parameters are\n1283\ttrainable, corresponding to 0.72%.\n1284\t\n1285\t{CONTEXT}\n1286\t\n1287\tUsing 200 training contexts sampled with seed 42, we\n1288\tcollect the last-input-token residual at every layer for both\n1289\tthe base and instructed prompts. At layer ℓ, the direction is\n1290\tuℓm =\n1291\t\n1292\tscore(xi ) = log PG (xi | x<i , ct ) + λ log PB (m | x≤i , ct ),\n1293\t\n1294\tDeAL. DeAL (Huang et al. 2025) is a decoding-time alignment baseline that performs an A∗ -style search—top-k expansion, greedy lookahead, and a self-reward heuristic—\n1295\ttoward the target move, without modifying weights or activations. It is text-only. Optionally the context is augmented\n1296\twith an alignment instruction pa ,\n1297\t\n1298\tYour next response should\n1299\t{DEFINITION}.\n1300\tRespond in a way that will\n1301\t{DEFINITION}.\n1302\tMake sure your reply will\n1303\t{DEFINITION}.\n1304\t\n1305\tℓ\n1306\tvm\n1307\t= µℓinstr,m − µℓbase ,\n1308\t\n1309\tAt decoding step i, the generator’s top K = 200 tokens\n1310\tare scored as\n1311\t\n1312\tℓ\n1313\tvm\n1314\t.\n1315\tℓ ∥\n1316\t∥vm\n1317\t2\n1318\t\n1319\tWe also store the target projection\n1320\tℓ\n1321\tz̄m\n1322\t= µℓinstr,m , uℓm .\n1323\t\n1324\tDuring generation, a projection-matching hook is applied\n1325\tat ℓ = ⌊L/2⌋. At each generated token, the intervention\n1326\tcoefficient is recomputed so that the residual’s projection\n1327\tℓ\n1328\tonto uℓm matches z̄m\n1329\t. We use gain 1.0 and greedy decoding.\n1330\tThe move instruction is used only to construct the direction\n1331\tand is not included in the generation prompt.\n1332\tFUDGE. FUDGE (Yang and Klein 2021) uses a\n1333\tlightweight future discriminator consisting of a 128dimensional token embedding, a single-layer LSTM with\n1334\thidden dimension 256, and a linear output layer over the\n1335\tfive moves. The discriminator is trained on response prefixes from the training dialogues to predict the completed\n1336\tresponse’s move. We apply class-balanced cross-entropy at\n1337\tevery prefix position and train using Adam with learning rate\n1338\t10−3 , batch size 64, and 8 epochs. Ten percent of the training dialogues are reserved for validation, and the checkpoint\n1339\twith the highest macro accuracy is retained.\n1340\t\n1341\tRespond with a {MOVE} move:\n1342\t{DEFINITION}\n1343\tbut in our main runs this instruction is dropped\n1344\t(-no-align-prompt) so that alignment is driven purely\n1345\tby the search reward and does not double-count the moveclassifier signal. At each committed step we (i) take the generator’s top K = 5 next-token candidates with their logprobabilities, (ii) extend each candidate with a greedy lookahead of L = 16 tokens, and (iii) score a self-reward\n1346\thj = log PG (m | ct , committed ⊕ candj ⊕ lookahead) ,\n1347\tread from the same generation model via A–E letter logprobabilities over the five moves. We commit\n1348\t\u0002\n1349\t\u0003\n1350\tȷ̂ = arg max log PG (candj ) + λ hj ,\n1351\tj\n1352\t\n1353\tadvancing stride = 4 tokens per decision, with λ = 0.5\n1354\tand at most 40 new tokens. Decoding is greedy; there is no\n1355\tsampling or beam search.\n1356\tPAS. PAS (Painless Activation Steering) is a selfsupervised, end-to-end baseline that builds a corrective steering vector without any external supervision (Cui and Chen\n1357\t2026). We run the unmodified generation model on the training split’s next-move-prediction task,\n1358\t\n1359\t\fThe follower’s possible moves are:\n1360\t- acknowledge: {DEFINITION}\n1361\t...\n1362\t- reply: {DEFINITION}\n1363\t\n1364\tFamily\n1365\t\n1366\tDialogue so far: {CONTEXT}\n1367\t\n1368\tQwen3Omni\n1369\t\n1370\tQwen/Qwen3-Omni30B-A3B-Instruct\n1371\t\n1372\tThe follower (F) speaks next. Which\n1373\tsingle move type will it be?\n1374\tAnswer\n1375\twith exactly one of: acknowledge,\n1376\tcheck, explain, query, reply. Answer:\n1377\t\n1378\tPhi-4\n1379\t\n1380\tmicrosoft/Phi-4- <|user|>,\n1381\tmultimodal-instruct<|assistant|>,\n1382\t<|end|>\n1383\t\n1384\tand partition up to 1,000 training contexts (seed 42) into correct and incorrect by the model’s own parsed answer versus\n1385\tthe gold move. The vector at layer ℓ is the raw difference of\n1386\tmean last-token residuals between a positive and a negative\n1387\tprompt set, captured at all layers,\n1388\t\u0001\n1389\t\u0001\n1390\ta∗ℓ = mean hℓ , last token − mean hℓ , last token .\n1391\tP+\n1392\t\n1393\tP−\n1394\t\n1395\tWe use three variants: PASf, whose P+ /P− are the full\n1396\tmultiple-choice prompts for correctly/incorrectly answered\n1397\tcontexts; iPASa, whose prompts append the model’s chosen move and are partitioned by correctness; and iPASwo,\n1398\tbuilt from incorrect contexts only, with P+ appending the\n1399\tcorrect move and P− the chosen wrong move (the default).\n1400\tAt generation the vector is added additively at a single layer\n1401\tℓ = ⌊L/2⌋ with strength λ = 1.0, using greedy decoding.\n1402\t\n1403\tCompute\n1404\tParameter\n1405\ttemperature\n1406\tnum_beams\n1407\ttop_k\n1408\ttop_p\n1409\trepetition_penalty\n1410\tmax_new_tokens\n1411\tstop_sequences\n1412\t\n1413\tValue\n1414\t0.0\n1415\t1\n1416\tdisabled (−1)\n1417\t1.0\n1418\t1.0\n1419\t8192\n1420\tnone\n1421\t\n1422\tTable 7: Hyperparameters used for decoding.\n1423\tWe use vLLM (Kwon et al. 2023) for inference. Parameters\n1424\tare shown in Table 7. All inference was run on Nvidia A40\n1425\tGPUs with 48GB GDDR6 memory. We use vLLM tensor\n1426\tparallelism across 2, 4, or 8 GPUs depending on model size.\n1427\tRuntime ranged from a few minutes to about one hour per\n1428\t(model, dataset) evaluation, depending on model size and\n1429\tdataset.\n1430\t\n1431\tModels\n1432\tWe evaluate three instruction-tuned multimodal language models: Qwen2.5-Omni, Qwen3-Omni, and Phi-4Multimodal, using the following Hugging Face checkpoints\n1433\twithout modification (Wolf et al. 2020).\n1434\t\n1435\tHugging Face Model ID Special tokens\n1436\t\n1437\tQwen2.5- Qwen/Qwen2.5Omni\n1438\tOmni-7B\n1439\t\n1440\t<|im_start|>,\n1441\t<|im_end|>,\n1442\t<eos>\n1443\t<|im_start|>,\n1444\t<|im_end|>,\n1445\t<eos>\n1446\t\n1447\tTable 8: Instruction-tuned models evaluated, with special\n1448\ttokens used for prompt construction.\n1449\t\n1450\tDatasets and Preprocessing\n1451\tCorpus Statistics and Splits\n1452\tWe evaluate on three task-oriented dialogue corpora in which\n1453\tone participant issues instructions—the giver, elder, or director—and the other executes them—the follower, helper,\n1454\tor searcher. We normalize these roles to giver and follower.\n1455\tThe follower turn is the unit of prediction and steering, and\n1456\teach retained follower turn constitutes one example.\n1457\tDataset\n1458\t\n1459\tDlg. Turns\n1460\t\n1461\tTrain/Test\n1462\t\n1463\tInput\n1464\t\n1465\tMapTask 128 11,581 8,876 / 2,705 Audio+text\n1466\tFindTask 133\n1467\t642\n1468\t486 / 156\n1469\tText\n1470\tCReST\n1471\t17 2,005 1,686 / 319 Audio+text\n1472\t\n1473\tTable 9: Dataset statistics after label mapping and filtering.\n1474\tSplits are grouped by dialogue. “Turns” counts retained follower turns whose labels map to one of the five target moves.\n1475\tAll experiments use a single canonical dialogue-grouped\n1476\tsplit. We hold out 20% of dialogues for testing, ensuring\n1477\tthat no dialogue appears in more than one split. Dialogue\n1478\tidentifiers are sorted and sampled without replacement using\n1479\tnumpy.default_rng(42), with\n1480\tntest = max(1, ⌊0.2|D|⌋) .\n1481\tThis produces 25 MapTask test dialogues, 26 FindTask test\n1482\tdialogues, and 3 CReST test dialogues.\n1483\tSteering-vector estimation, controller training, baseline\n1484\ttraining, and end-to-end evaluation all use this split. No separate development set is reserved for the main experiments.\n1485\tWhen an auxiliary validation set is needed, we sample 10%\n1486\tof the training dialogues: seed 42 for the FUDGE discriminator and seed 123 for the controller.\n1487\t\n1488\tMove-Label Mapping\n1489\tWe map corpus-specific dialogue-act labels into the shared\n1490\tmove set\n1491\tM = {acknowledge, check, explain, query, reply}.\n1492\tOnly turns mapped to these five moves are used as prediction\n1493\tor generation targets. Excluded turns remain in the preceding\n1494\tdialogue context unless otherwise noted.\n1495\t\n1496\t\fDataset\n1497\t\n1498\tExplain\n1499\t\n1500\tQuery\n1501\t\n1502\tMapTask acknowledge, ready check\n1503\t\n1504\texplain, clarify\n1505\t\n1506\tFindTask acknowledge\n1507\t\n1508\texplain\n1509\t\n1510\tquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\n1511\treply_w\n1512\tquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\n1513\treply_w\n1514\tquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\n1515\treply_w, confirm\n1516\t\n1517\tCReST\n1518\t\n1519\tAcknowledge\n1520\t\n1521\tCheck\n1522\t\n1523\tcheck\n1524\t\n1525\tacknowledge, ready, check\n1526\tagree, continue\n1527\t\n1528\texplain, clarify,\n1529\tassessment\n1530\t\n1531\tReply\n1532\t\n1533\tExcluded\n1534\talign, uncodable\n1535\talign, state, state_y,\n1536\tstate_n, instruct\n1537\talign, instruct,\n1538\tstate_change,\n1539\tincomplete, open,\n1540\tclose, 3rd_turn,\n1541\tgreeting, read,\n1542\talarm\n1543\t\n1544\tTable 10: Mapping from corpus-specific dialogue-act labels to the shared five-move taxonomy. Excluded labels are not used as\n1545\ttarget turns but are retained as context unless removed by the preprocessing rules below.\n1546\t\n1547\tController Details\n1548\t\n1549\tText and Audio Preprocessing\n1550\tTurn filtering. We remove giver, elder, and director turns\n1551\tfrom the target set, although they remain available as dialogue\n1552\tcontext. We additionally exclude turns with empty text and\n1553\tfollower turns with no preceding context. MapTask turns\n1554\tlabeled uncodable are removed.\n1555\tFor CReST, filler-only turns are removed from both the\n1556\ttarget set and dialogue context:\n1557\t{um, uh, and, un, wait, whoops, and_um}.\n1558\tWe also repair known transcription errors, remove inline\n1559\tannotations enclosed in braces, brackets, or parentheses, and\n1560\tuse the primary label preceding “/” when multiple tags are\n1561\tpresent.\n1562\tAudio processing. MapTask and CReST use audio and\n1563\ttranscript input, while FindTask is text-only. Dialogue waveforms are loaded from <id>.mix.wav, converted to mono,\n1564\tand resampled to 16 kHz.\n1565\tFor generation, the audio segment begins 0.2 s before the\n1566\tonset of the immediately preceding turn and ends at the\n1567\tonset of the target follower response, ensuring that the heldout response is never included. Segments are capped at 30 s,\n1568\tretaining the most recent audio when the available context is\n1569\tlonger. For teacher-forced activation extraction, the segment\n1570\textends through the end of the gold follower response.\n1571\tWhen a waveform is unavailable, we substitute 0.1 s of silence to preserve the expected number of audio placeholders.\n1572\tPrompt construction. For multimodal conditions, the user\n1573\tinput contains the audio item followed by the corresponding\n1574\ttranscript. Transcript context contains the most recent n turns\n1575\twhen a context limit is specified and the full preceding dialogue otherwise. Audio+text and text-only conditions are\n1576\tstored and evaluated separately. The automatic move classifier always receives text-only input.\n1577\tSpeaker normalization. Corpus-specific roles are rendered in generation prompts using G: for the giver role and\n1578\tF: for the follower role. For automatic classification, these\n1579\tprefixes are further anonymized:\n1580\tG: → A:,\n1581\tF: → B:.\n1582\tThe speaker prefix is removed from the final response being\n1583\tclassified, reducing dataset- and role-specific cues.\n1584\t\n1585\tFrozen spoken dialogue LM\n1586\tht\n1587\t\n1588\tper-turn pool xt ∈ Rd\n1589\t\n1590\tselective SSM zt → ot\n1591\t\n1592\tnext turn\n1593\t\n1594\tmove ŷt+1\n1595\t\n1596\tb t+1\n1597\tlength L\n1598\t\n1599\tdirection selection, gating,\n1600\tand strength calibration\n1601\t\n1602\tsteer: vm @ ℓ⋆m + veot @ ℓ⋆eot\n1603\t\n1604\tFigure 4: The controller reads the frozen backbone’s residual\n1605\tstream and predicts the next conversational move and response length. The predicted move selects a move direction,\n1606\tits fixed steering strength, and the realization-aware gate; the\n1607\tpredicted length is calibrated to a turn-boundary coefficient.\n1608\tAll decisions depend on controller predictions rather than\n1609\tgold labels at inference.\n1610\tPer-turn feature. For each dialogue turn t, we mean-pool\n1611\tthe residual-stream activations at controller layer ℓ⋆ctrl over\n1612\tthe turn’s token span and standardize them using training-set\n1613\tstatistics:\n1614\t\u0010 \u0010 ⋆ \u0011\n1615\t\u0011\n1616\tℓ\n1617\txt = ϕ ht ctrl − µ ⊘ σ ∈ Rd ,\n1618\t(9)\n1619\twhere ϕ denotes mean pooling, (µ, σ) are the per-coordinate\n1620\ttraining-set mean and standard deviation, and ⊘ denotes elementwise division.\n1621\t\n1622\t\fObjective. For each retained follower turn t, the causal\n1623\tstate after turn t − 1 predicts its move and log response\n1624\tlength:\n1625\tX \u0010\n1626\tCE(Wo ot−1 , yt )\n1627\tL=\n1628\tt∈Itrain\n1629\t⊤\n1630\t+ λ smoothL1 wL\n1631\tot−1 , Lt\n1632\t\n1633\t\u0001\u0011\n1634\t\n1635\t,\n1636\t\n1637\t(10)\n1638\t\n1639\twhere\n1640\tLt = log(1 + words(ut )) ,\n1641\tand Itrain indexes retained training-set follower turns whose\n1642\tlabels belong to M.\n1643\tLength calibration.\n1644\t\n1645\tAt inference, the predicted log length\n1646\t⊤\n1647\tb t+1 = wL\n1648\tL\n1649\tot\n1650\t\n1651\tin standardized-PCA coordinates. We map it back to\n1652\traw activation space, undoing the PCA rotation and the\n1653\tper-dimension scaling,\n1654\t\u0001\n1655\tṽ ℓ\n1656\tℓ\n1657\tℓ\n1658\tℓ\n1659\tṽm\n1660\t= diag (σ ℓ )−1 (P ℓ )⊤ wm\n1661\t,\n1662\tvm\n1663\t= ℓm , (11)\n1664\t∥ṽm ∥2\n1665\twhere P ℓ are the PCA components and σ ℓ the per-dimension\n1666\tstandard deviations of the standardizer (elementwise division). The result is unit-normed in the raw residual space\n1667\t(d = 3584 for Qwen2.5-Omni). The vector is oriented toward\n1668\tthe target move by construction (LDA class 1 = move), so no\n1669\tseparate sign-fixing rule is applied. Directions are estimated\n1670\tindependently per backbone.\n1671\tInjection. Steering is applied additively to the post-block\n1672\tresidual stream at the selected move-specific layer. For every\n1673\tgenerated token position s,\n1674\tℓ⋆\n1675\t\n1676\tis mapped to a turn-boundary steering coefficient β by a\n1677\tmonotone calibration fitted on the training dialogues. The coefficient is applied only to naturally terse moves. For contentbearing moves (check and explain), it is set to zero because\n1678\tshortening strong enough to affect these moves can overwhelm the move direction.\n1679\t\n1680\t⋆\n1681\tHere, αm\n1682\tis the selected move-specific steering coefficient\n1683\tand cℓ⋆m is the median L2 norm of the residual stream at\n1684\tlayer ℓ⋆m , estimated over generated tokens from 300 MapTask\n1685\t⋆\n1686\tdimensiontraining contexts. This normalization makes αm\n1687\t\n1688\tStreaming inference. At deployment, the controller performs one incremental update\n1689\t\n1690\tless. The intervention is applied only during decoding, not\n1691\tto prompt-prefill positions, and uses constant strength across\n1692\tgenerated tokens with no positional decay.\n1693\t\n1694\t(ot , zt ) = step(xt , zt−1 )\n1695\tper dialogue turn. Its recurrent state persists across turns, so\n1696\tthe controller does not recompute its previous states. The update has constant time and memory with respect to dialogue\n1697\thistory and is equivalent to the parallel scan used during\n1698\ttraining, up to a numerical difference below 10−7 . Thus,\n1699\tthe controller operates as a streaming policy rather than an\n1700\toffline classifier.\n1701\t\n1702\tSteering Implementation\n1703\tOnset features. For each retained follower turn t ∈ Itrain ,\n1704\twe extract the move-onset delta\n1705\t∆ℓt = hℓt (st,1 ) − hℓt (st,1 − 1),\n1706\tthe change in the layer-ℓ residual stream between the positions immediately before and at the first response token\n1707\t(Eq. 3). Activations are extracted under teacher forcing. For\n1708\teach target move m, we form a balanced one-vs-rest set containing all turns labeled m and an equal-size random sample\n1709\tof the remaining retained follower turns.\n1710\tDirection estimation (deployed). The deployed steering\n1711\tdirection is the PCA-reduced, shrinkage-LDA (Fisher)\n1712\tdirection of Eq. 4, mapped back to the raw residual stream.\n1713\tOn the balanced one-vs-rest set we (i) standardize the onset\n1714\tfeatures per dimension (StandardScaler), (ii) reduce to\n1715\tk = min(128, n−1, d) principal components, and (iii) fit\n1716\tLinearDiscriminantAnalysis(solver=\"lsqr\",\n1717\tshrinkage=\"auto\"), whose lsqr coefficient vector\n1718\tb −1 (ν ℓ − ν ℓ ) with the within-class\n1719\tis proportional to Σ\n1720\tw\n1721\tm\n1722\t¬m\n1723\tb w regularized by automatic Ledoit–Wolf\n1724\tcovariance Σ\n1725\tℓ\n1726\tshrinkage (Ledoit and Wolf 2004). Let wm\n1727\tbe this direction\n1728\t\n1729\t⋆\n1730\t\n1731\t⋆\n1732\t\n1733\t⋆\n1734\thℓm (s) ← hℓm (s) + αm\n1735\tcℓ⋆m vmm .\n1736\t\n1737\t(12)\n1738\t\n1739\tLayer and strength selection. For each move m and back⋆\n1740\tusing only MapTask training\n1741\tbone, we select ℓ⋆m and αm\n1742\tdialogues through a two-stage sweep. Stage 1 evaluates all\n1743\tlayers at a fixed α = 0.1 and selects the layer maximizing\n1744\tthe mean paired soft lift\n1745\ttrain [Psteered (m | c) − Pbase (m | c)] ,\n1746\tEc∈C¬m\n1747\t\n1748\ttrain\n1749\tcontains MapTask training contexts whose gold\n1750\twhere C¬m\n1751\tmove is not m, and P is obtained from the Qwen2.5-72B\n1752\tmove classifier. Stage 2 fixes the selected layer and sweeps\n1753\tα ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4}, choosing the value with the\n1754\tgreatest soft lift among those whose degenerate-generation\n1755\trate is at most 0.10. Directions and hyperparameters are\n1756\testimated independently for each backbone. The resulting\n1757\tℓ⋆\n1758\t⋆\n1759\tare then transferred unchanged to held-out\n1760\tvmm , ℓ⋆m , and αm\n1761\tMapTask, FindTask, and CReST.\n1762\t\n1763\tAdaptive strength. As an optional variant, we modulate\n1764\tthe move-steering strength for each example using its alignment with the target direction:\n1765\t⋆\n1766\tscale = αm\n1767\tcℓ⋆m (1 + γzc,m ) ,\n1768\t\n1769\twhere zc,m is the alignment of the context representation at\n1770\tℓ⋆\n1771\tℓ⋆m with vmm , standardized using the training-set mean and\n1772\tstandard deviation. The coefficient γ controls the adaptive\n1773\tgain; γ = 0 recovers the fixed-strength intervention.\n1774\t\n1775\tTurn-Boundary Subspace and\n1776\tRealization-Aware Gating\n1777\tPredicted length.\n1778\tturn’s log length\n1779\t\n1780\tThe controller regresses the follower\n1781\t\n1782\tLt+1 = log(1 + words(ut+1 )) .\n1783\t\n1784\t\fPredicted Move\n1785\tacknowledge\n1786\tcheck\n1787\texplain\n1788\tquery\n1789\treply\n1790\t\n1791\tMove Steering\n1792\tapply\n1793\tapply\n1794\tapply\n1795\tapply\n1796\twithhold\n1797\t\n1798\tEOT Steering\n1799\tapply\n1800\twithhold\n1801\twithhold\n1802\tapply\n1803\tapply\n1804\t\n1805\tDataset\n1806\t\n1807\tPlurality Unanimous\n1808\tAgreement Agreement\n1809\t\n1810\tMapTask\n1811\tFindTask\n1812\tCReST\n1813\t\n1814\t76.4%\n1815\t67.8%\n1816\t75.5%\n1817\t\n1818\t94.6%\n1819\t91.9%\n1820\t86.5%\n1821\t\n1822\tOverall\n1823\t\n1824\t73.9%\n1825\t\n1826\t90.5%\n1827\t\n1828\tTable 11: Realization-aware gating policy.\n1829\tAt inference, the prediction\n1830\t⊤\n1831\tb t+1 = wL\n1832\tL\n1833\tot\n1834\t\n1835\tis mapped to the turn-boundary coefficient β by a monotone\n1836\tcalibration fitted on training dialogues.\n1837\t\n1838\tHuman Study\n1839\tWe conduct a human study to validate the automatic move\n1840\tclassifier against human judgment on model-generated responses, and to measure agreement between human annotators and the LLM-based evaluator. From the pooled generations of all methods, we sample 500 responses—100 for each\n1841\tof the five target moves, distributed approximately equally\n1842\tacross the three generation models and three datasets. Each\n1843\tresponse is annotated by three workers who see only the dialogue context and the generated utterance and select one\n1844\tconversational move; the target move, generation method,\n1845\tand classifier prediction are withheld so that annotation is\n1846\tblind.\n1847\tAnnotators. All annotations were collected on Amazon\n1848\tMechanical Turk (AMT) using workers with a minimum\n1849\tapproval rate of 95%. Annotators were restricted to Englishspeaking countries to ensure reliable comprehension. Compensation was set to meet or exceed the U.S. federal minimum wage based on conservative task-time estimates. No\n1850\tpersonally identifiable information was collected.\n1851\t\n1852\tHuman–LLM Agreement\n1853\tWe compare human judgments to the LLM move classifier\n1854\tat the item level. Human decisions are aggregated by plurality voting across the three annotators, and the LLM prediction is the label assigned by the two-stage Qwen2.5-72BInstruct classifier (Yang et al. 2025). Agreement is reported\n1855\tper dataset in Table 12: overall, the classifier matches the\n1856\thuman plurality on 73.9% of items, rising to 90.5% on the\n1857\t327 items where all three annotators agreed. Inter-annotator\n1858\tagreement is moderate (Fleiss’ κ = 0.682 overall), and the\n1859\tclassifier’s agreement with the human majority is comparable\n1860\tto the agreement among annotators themselves.\n1861\tTo assess alignment at the distribution level, we compare\n1862\tthe classifier’s normalized move probabilities with the human\n1863\tvote shares across all item–move pairs. The two are positively correlated (Pearson r = 0.721 overall, ranging from\n1864\tr = 0.62 for explain to r = 0.85 for query), indicating that\n1865\tthe classifier’s confidence tracks human uncertainty. Moreover, ranking the seven generation methods by human-judged\n1866\ttarget-move realization reproduces the classifier-based ranking almost exactly (Spearman ρ = 0.96, Pearson r = 0.99),\n1867\t\n1868\tTable 12: Agreement between the automatic classifier and\n1869\thuman judgments. Plurality agreement uses items with a\n1870\tclear majority; unanimous agreement uses only items on\n1871\twhich all three annotators selected the same move.\n1872\t\n1873\tshowing that the two evaluators agree not only per item but\n1874\talso in their relative assessment of methods. These results\n1875\tsupport the use of the LLM move classifier as a scalable\n1876\tproxy for human judgment in our evaluation.\n1877\t\n1878\tMTurk Interfaces\n1879\tFigure 5 shows the annotation interface presented to workers.\n1880\t\n1881\t\fFigure 5: Human annotation interface for move classification. Annotators are shown the preceding dialogue context and a single\n1882\tcandidate response, and asked to select the conversational move the response performs (acknowledge, check, explain, query, or\n1883\treply). The target move, generation method, and classifier prediction are hidden.\n1884\t\n1885\t\f\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "568874b8-8b57-4156-a4b8-cd5669c4cba7", "uuid": "d1bfab7c-ec86-4ecc-a8db-552e98b75d44", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T08:39:41.331Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/latent_im.txt", "content": "\nThe preceding turn was labeled: {PRECEDING_MOVE}\n\nAutomatic Move Classifier\n\nFinal turn to classify:\n“{MODEL_RESPONSE}”\n\nThe automatic evaluator classifies the generated follower turn\ninto one of five moves:\n\nAnswer with exactly one word from: acknowledge,\ncheck, explain, query, or reply.\n\nM = {acknowledge, check, explain, query, reply}.\n\nAnswer:\n\nDecoding is constrained to these five label tokens. To remove\ndataset-specific cues, speaker prefixes are normalized to A\nand B. The classifier receives the immediately preceding\nturn, its move label when available, and the follower response\nbeing evaluated.\nSystem:\nTwo people are doing a task together by talking. Read the\nrecent exchange and classify the FINAL turn by what it\nis doing in the conversation. Choose exactly one:\nacknowledge – only signals that the speaker heard or\nregistered the other; adds no new information and asks\nnothing (“okay,” “mm-hmm,” “right,” “uh-huh,” “I see”).\ncheck – confirms something already said or inferred by\nrestating, paraphrasing, or pointing to it (“so I go left?”,\n“the blue one?”, “this one?”).\n\nBaselines\nPrompt-Based Learning, Steering-Instruct, and FUDGE receive the target move in the oracle-realization setting. SFT\nis an end-to-end baseline and receives no move label during\ntraining or inference.\nPrompt-Based Learning (PBL). PBL (Ramirez et al.\n2023) prepends N = 10 in-context demonstrations sampled\nfrom the training split using seed 42. Each demonstration\ncontains at most the three most recent context turns. Demonstrations are sampled at run time rather than drawn from a\nsingle fixed set.\nThe default target-shaped template is:\nHere is a dialogue context:\n{CONTEXT}\n\n\fThe follower’s next response,\nwritten as a ‘{MOVE}’ dialogue act:\n{RESPONSE}\nAt test time, the same template is used but ends immediately after dialogue act:. The definitional variant prepends the target move’s definition. The fewshot\nvariant instead uses:\nContext: {CONTEXT}\nResponse ({MOVE}): {RESPONSE}\nFor each example, the model samples K = 10 candidates\nusing temperature 0.7, top-p = 1.0, and at most 60 new\ntokens. Among candidates that the move classifier assigns to\nthe target move, PBL selects the one with the highest mean\ntoken log-probability. If no candidate is assigned to the target\nmove, it selects\nŜ = arg max Pcls (m | ct , S) PG (S | ct ).\nS\n\nSteering-Instruct. Steering-Instruct (Stolfo et al. 2025)\ncontrasts a bare dialogue context against three moveconditioned prompt variants:\n\nwhere λ = 1. The highest-scoring token is selected greedily,\nwithout sampling or beam search.\nSupervised Fine-Tuning (SFT). SFT is an end-to-end\nbaseline that fine-tunes the backbone on context–response\npairs,\nct −→ ut ,\nwithout providing a move label during training or inference\n(Ouyang et al. 2022).\nWe apply LoRA with rank r = 16, scaling parameter\n32, dropout 0.05, and all linear layers as target modules\n(Hu et al. 2022). Training is text-only and uses up to 1,000\ntraining pairs, with loss computed only over response tokens.\nWe use AdamW with learning rate 2 × 10−4 , three epochs,\nper-device batch size 1, gradient accumulation over 16 steps,\nBF16 precision, and seed 42. The final-epoch weights are\nretained without validation-based checkpoint selection. For\nQwen2.5-Omni, approximately 65M of 9.0B parameters are\ntrainable, corresponding to 0.72%.\n\n{CONTEXT}\n\nUsing 200 training contexts sampled with seed 42, we\ncollect the last-input-token residual at every layer for both\nthe base and instructed prompts. At layer ℓ, the direction is\nuℓm =\n\nscore(xi ) = log PG (xi | x<i , ct ) + λ log PB (m | x≤i , ct ),\n\nDeAL. DeAL (Huang et al. 2025) is a decoding-time alignment baseline that performs an A∗ -style search—top-k expansion, greedy lookahead, and a self-reward heuristic—\ntoward the target move, without modifying weights or activations. It is text-only. Optionally the context is augmented\nwith an alignment instruction pa ,\n\nYour next response should\n{DEFINITION}.\nRespond in a way that will\n{DEFINITION}.\nMake sure your reply will\n{DEFINITION}.\n\nℓ\nvm\n= µℓinstr,m − µℓbase ,\n\nAt decoding step i, the generator’s top K = 200 tokens\nare scored as\n\nℓ\nvm\n.\nℓ ∥\n∥vm\n2\n\nWe also store the target projection\nℓ\nz̄m\n= µℓinstr,m , uℓm .\n\nDuring generation, a projection-matching hook is applied\nat ℓ = ⌊L/2⌋. At each generated token, the intervention\ncoefficient is recomputed so that the residual’s projection\nℓ\nonto uℓm matches z̄m\n. We use gain 1.0 and greedy decoding.\nThe move instruction is used only to construct the direction\nand is not included in the generation prompt.\nFUDGE. FUDGE (Yang and Klein 2021) uses a\nlightweight future discriminator consisting of a 128dimensional token embedding, a single-layer LSTM with\nhidden dimension 256, and a linear output layer over the\nfive moves. The discriminator is trained on response prefixes from the training dialogues to predict the completed\nresponse’s move. We apply class-balanced cross-entropy at\nevery prefix position and train using Adam with learning rate\n10−3 , batch size 64, and 8 epochs. Ten percent of the training dialogues are reserved for validation, and the checkpoint\nwith the highest macro accuracy is retained.\n\nRespond with a {MOVE} move:\n{DEFINITION}\nbut in our main runs this instruction is dropped\n(-no-align-prompt) so that alignment is driven purely\nby the search reward and does not double-count the moveclassifier signal. At each committed step we (i) take the generator’s top K = 5 next-token candidates with their logprobabilities, (ii) extend each candidate with a greedy lookahead of L = 16 tokens, and (iii) score a self-reward\nhj = log PG (m | ct , committed ⊕ candj ⊕ lookahead) ,\nread from the same generation model via A–E letter logprobabilities over the five moves. We commit\n\u0002\n\u0003\nȷ̂ = arg max log PG (candj ) + λ hj ,\nj\n\nadvancing stride = 4 tokens per decision, with λ = 0.5\nand at most 40 new tokens. Decoding is greedy; there is no\nsampling or beam search.\nPAS. PAS (Painless Activation Steering) is a selfsupervised, end-to-end baseline that builds a corrective steering vector without any external supervision (Cui and Chen\n2026). We run the unmodified generation model on the training split’s next-move-prediction task,\n\n\fThe follower’s possible moves are:\n- acknowledge: {DEFINITION}\n...\n- reply: {DEFINITION}\n\nFamily\n\nDialogue so far: {CONTEXT}\n\nQwen3Omni\n\nQwen/Qwen3-Omni30B-A3B-Instruct\n\nThe follower (F) speaks next. Which\nsingle move type will it be?\nAnswer\nwith exactly one of: acknowledge,\ncheck, explain, query, reply. Answer:\n\nPhi-4\n\nmicrosoft/Phi-4- <|user|>,\nmultimodal-instruct<|assistant|>,\n<|end|>\n\nand partition up to 1,000 training contexts (seed 42) into correct and incorrect by the model’s own parsed answer versus\nthe gold move. The vector at layer ℓ is the raw difference of\nmean last-token residuals between a positive and a negative\nprompt set, captured at all layers,\n\u0001\n\u0001\na∗ℓ = mean hℓ , last token − mean hℓ , last token .\nP+\n\nP−\n\nWe use three variants: PASf, whose P+ /P− are the full\nmultiple-choice prompts for correctly/incorrectly answered\ncontexts; iPASa, whose prompts append the model’s chosen move and are partitioned by correctness; and iPASwo,\nbuilt from incorrect contexts only, with P+ appending the\ncorrect move and P− the chosen wrong move (the default).\nAt generation the vector is added additively at a single layer\nℓ = ⌊L/2⌋ with strength λ = 1.0, using greedy decoding.\n\nCompute\nParameter\ntemperature\nnum_beams\ntop_k\ntop_p\nrepetition_penalty\nmax_new_tokens\nstop_sequences\n\nValue\n0.0\n1\ndisabled (−1)\n1.0\n1.0\n8192\nnone\n\nTable 7: Hyperparameters used for decoding.\nWe use vLLM (Kwon et al. 2023) for inference. Parameters\nare shown in Table 7. All inference was run on Nvidia A40\nGPUs with 48GB GDDR6 memory. We use vLLM tensor\nparallelism across 2, 4, or 8 GPUs depending on model size.\nRuntime ranged from a few minutes to about one hour per\n(model, dataset) evaluation, depending on model size and\ndataset.\n\nModels\nWe evaluate three instruction-tuned multimodal language models: Qwen2.5-Omni, Qwen3-Omni, and Phi-4Multimodal, using the following Hugging Face checkpoints\nwithout modification (Wolf et al. 2020).\n\nHugging Face Model ID Special tokens\n\nQwen2.5- Qwen/Qwen2.5Omni\nOmni-7B\n\n<|im_start|>,\n<|im_end|>,\n<eos>\n<|im_start|>,\n<|im_end|>,\n<eos>\n\nTable 8: Instruction-tuned models evaluated, with special\ntokens used for prompt construction.\n\nDatasets and Preprocessing\nCorpus Statistics and Splits\nWe evaluate on three task-oriented dialogue corpora in which\none participant issues instructions—the giver, elder, or director—and the other executes them—the follower, helper,\nor searcher. We normalize these roles to giver and follower.\nThe follower turn is the unit of prediction and steering, and\neach retained follower turn constitutes one example.\nDataset\n\nDlg. Turns\n\nTrain/Test\n\nInput\n\nMapTask 128 11,581 8,876 / 2,705 Audio+text\nFindTask 133\n642\n486 / 156\nText\nCReST\n17 2,005 1,686 / 319 Audio+text\n\nTable 9: Dataset statistics after label mapping and filtering.\nSplits are grouped by dialogue. “Turns” counts retained follower turns whose labels map to one of the five target moves.\nAll experiments use a single canonical dialogue-grouped\nsplit. We hold out 20% of dialogues for testing, ensuring\nthat no dialogue appears in more than one split. Dialogue\nidentifiers are sorted and sampled without replacement using\nnumpy.default_rng(42), with\nntest = max(1, ⌊0.2|D|⌋) .\nThis produces 25 MapTask test dialogues, 26 FindTask test\ndialogues, and 3 CReST test dialogues.\nSteering-vector estimation, controller training, baseline\ntraining, and end-to-end evaluation all use this split. No separate development set is reserved for the main experiments.\nWhen an auxiliary validation set is needed, we sample 10%\nof the training dialogues: seed 42 for the FUDGE discriminator and seed 123 for the controller.\n\nMove-Label Mapping\nWe map corpus-specific dialogue-act labels into the shared\nmove set\nM = {acknowledge, check, explain, query, reply}.\nOnly turns mapped to these five moves are used as prediction\nor generation targets. Excluded turns remain in the preceding\ndialogue context unless otherwise noted.\n\n\fDataset\n\nExplain\n\nQuery\n\nMapTask acknowledge, ready check\n\nexplain, clarify\n\nFindTask acknowledge\n\nexplain\n\nquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\nreply_w\nquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\nreply_w\nquery_yn, query_w reply_y, reply_n,\nreply_w, confirm\n\nCReST\n\nAcknowledge\n\nCheck\n\ncheck\n\nacknowledge, ready, check\nagree, continue\n\nexplain, clarify,\nassessment\n\nReply\n\nExcluded\nalign, uncodable\nalign, state, state_y,\nstate_n, instruct\nalign, instruct,\nstate_change,\nincomplete, open,\nclose, 3rd_turn,\ngreeting, read,\nalarm\n\nTable 10: Mapping from corpus-specific dialogue-act labels to the shared five-move taxonomy. Excluded labels are not used as\ntarget turns but are retained as context unless removed by the preprocessing rules below.\n\nController Details\n\nText and Audio Preprocessing\nTurn filtering. We remove giver, elder, and director turns\nfrom the target set, although they remain available as dialogue\ncontext. We additionally exclude turns with empty text and\nfollower turns with no preceding context. MapTask turns\nlabeled uncodable are removed.\nFor CReST, filler-only turns are removed from both the\ntarget set and dialogue context:\n{um, uh, and, un, wait, whoops, and_um}.\nWe also repair known transcription errors, remove inline\nannotations enclosed in braces, brackets, or parentheses, and\nuse the primary label preceding “/” when multiple tags are\npresent.\nAudio processing. MapTask and CReST use audio and\ntranscript input, while FindTask is text-only. Dialogue waveforms are loaded from <id>.mix.wav, converted to mono,\nand resampled to 16 kHz.\nFor generation, the audio segment begins 0.2 s before the\nonset of the immediately preceding turn and ends at the\nonset of the target follower response, ensuring that the heldout response is never included. Segments are capped at 30 s,\nretaining the most recent audio when the available context is\nlonger. For teacher-forced activation extraction, the segment\nextends through the end of the gold follower response.\nWhen a waveform is unavailable, we substitute 0.1 s of silence to preserve the expected number of audio placeholders.\nPrompt construction. For multimodal conditions, the user\ninput contains the audio item followed by the corresponding\ntranscript. Transcript context contains the most recent n turns\nwhen a context limit is specified and the full preceding dialogue otherwise. Audio+text and text-only conditions are\nstored and evaluated separately. The automatic move classifier always receives text-only input.\nSpeaker normalization. Corpus-specific roles are rendered in generation prompts using G: for the giver role and\nF: for the follower role. For automatic classification, these\nprefixes are further anonymized:\nG: → A:,\nF: → B:.\nThe speaker prefix is removed from the final response being\nclassified, reducing dataset- and role-specific cues.\n\nFrozen spoken dialogue LM\nht\n\nper-turn pool xt ∈ Rd\n\nselective SSM zt → ot\n\nnext turn\n\nmove ŷt+1\n\nb t+1\nlength L\n\ndirection selection, gating,\nand strength calibration\n\nsteer: vm @ ℓ⋆m + veot @ ℓ⋆eot\n\nFigure 4: The controller reads the frozen backbone’s residual\nstream and predicts the next conversational move and response length. The predicted move selects a move direction,\nits fixed steering strength, and the realization-aware gate; the\npredicted length is calibrated to a turn-boundary coefficient.\nAll decisions depend on controller predictions rather than\ngold labels at inference.\nPer-turn feature. For each dialogue turn t, we mean-pool\nthe residual-stream activations at controller layer ℓ⋆ctrl over\nthe turn’s token span and standardize them using training-set\nstatistics:\n\u0010 \u0010 ⋆ \u0011\n\u0011\nℓ\nxt = ϕ ht ctrl − µ ⊘ σ ∈ Rd ,\n(9)\nwhere ϕ denotes mean pooling, (µ, σ) are the per-coordinate\ntraining-set mean and standard deviation, and ⊘ denotes elementwise division.\n\n\fObjective. For each retained follower turn t, the causal\nstate after turn t − 1 predicts its move and log response\nlength:\nX \u0010\nCE(Wo ot−1 , yt )\nL=\nt∈Itrain\n⊤\n+ λ smoothL1 wL\not−1 , Lt\n\n\u0001\u0011\n\n,\n\n(10)\n\nwhere\nLt = log(1 + words(ut )) ,\nand Itrain indexes retained training-set follower turns whose\nlabels belong to M.\nLength calibration.\n\nAt inference, the predicted log length\n⊤\nb t+1 = wL\nL\not\n\nin standardized-PCA coordinates. We map it back to\nraw activation space, undoing the PCA rotation and the\nper-dimension scaling,\n\u0001\nṽ ℓ\nℓ\nℓ\nℓ\nṽm\n= diag (σ ℓ )−1 (P ℓ )⊤ wm\n,\nvm\n= ℓm , (11)\n∥ṽm ∥2\nwhere P ℓ are the PCA components and σ ℓ the per-dimension\nstandard deviations of the standardizer (elementwise division). The result is unit-normed in the raw residual space\n(d = 3584 for Qwen2.5-Omni). The vector is oriented toward\nthe target move by construction (LDA class 1 = move), so no\nseparate sign-fixing rule is applied. Directions are estimated\nindependently per backbone.\nInjection. Steering is applied additively to the post-block\nresidual stream at the selected move-specific layer. For every\ngenerated token position s,\nℓ⋆\n\nis mapped to a turn-boundary steering coefficient β by a\nmonotone calibration fitted on the training dialogues. The coefficient is applied only to naturally terse moves. For contentbearing moves (check and explain), it is set to zero because\nshortening strong enough to affect these moves can overwhelm the move direction.\n\n⋆\nHere, αm\nis the selected move-specific steering coefficient\nand cℓ⋆m is the median L2 norm of the residual stream at\nlayer ℓ⋆m , estimated over generated tokens from 300 MapTask\n⋆\ndimensiontraining contexts. This normalization makes αm\n\nStreaming inference. At deployment, the controller performs one incremental update\n\nless. The intervention is applied only during decoding, not\nto prompt-prefill positions, and uses constant strength across\ngenerated tokens with no positional decay.\n\n(ot , zt ) = step(xt , zt−1 )\nper dialogue turn. Its recurrent state persists across turns, so\nthe controller does not recompute its previous states. The update has constant time and memory with respect to dialogue\nhistory and is equivalent to the parallel scan used during\ntraining, up to a numerical difference below 10−7 . Thus,\nthe controller operates as a streaming policy rather than an\noffline classifier.\n\nSteering Implementation\nOnset features. For each retained follower turn t ∈ Itrain ,\nwe extract the move-onset delta\n∆ℓt = hℓt (st,1 ) − hℓt (st,1 − 1),\nthe change in the layer-ℓ residual stream between the positions immediately before and at the first response token\n(Eq. 3). Activations are extracted under teacher forcing. For\neach target move m, we form a balanced one-vs-rest set containing all turns labeled m and an equal-size random sample\nof the remaining retained follower turns.\nDirection estimation (deployed). The deployed steering\ndirection is the PCA-reduced, shrinkage-LDA (Fisher)\ndirection of Eq. 4, mapped back to the raw residual stream.\nOn the balanced one-vs-rest set we (i) standardize the onset\nfeatures per dimension (StandardScaler), (ii) reduce to\nk = min(128, n−1, d) principal components, and (iii) fit\nLinearDiscriminantAnalysis(solver=\"lsqr\",\nshrinkage=\"auto\"), whose lsqr coefficient vector\nb −1 (ν ℓ − ν ℓ ) with the within-class\nis proportional to Σ\nw\nm\n¬m\nb w regularized by automatic Ledoit–Wolf\ncovariance Σ\nℓ\nshrinkage (Ledoit and Wolf 2004). Let wm\nbe this direction\n\n⋆\n\n⋆\n\n⋆\nhℓm (s) ← hℓm (s) + αm\ncℓ⋆m vmm .\n\n(12)\n\nLayer and strength selection. For each move m and back⋆\nusing only MapTask training\nbone, we select ℓ⋆m and αm\ndialogues through a two-stage sweep. Stage 1 evaluates all\nlayers at a fixed α = 0.1 and selects the layer maximizing\nthe mean paired soft lift\ntrain [Psteered (m | c) − Pbase (m | c)] ,\nEc∈C¬m\n\ntrain\ncontains MapTask training contexts whose gold\nwhere C¬m\nmove is not m, and P is obtained from the Qwen2.5-72B\nmove classifier. Stage 2 fixes the selected layer and sweeps\nα ∈ {0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4}, choosing the value with the\ngreatest soft lift among those whose degenerate-generation\nrate is at most 0.10. Directions and hyperparameters are\nestimated independently for each backbone. The resulting\nℓ⋆\n⋆\nare then transferred unchanged to held-out\nvmm , ℓ⋆m , and αm\nMapTask, FindTask, and CReST.\n\nAdaptive strength. As an optional variant, we modulate\nthe move-steering strength for each example using its alignment with the target direction:\n⋆\nscale = αm\ncℓ⋆m (1 + γzc,m ) ,\n\nwhere zc,m is the alignment of the context representation at\nℓ⋆\nℓ⋆m with vmm , standardized using the training-set mean and\nstandard deviation. The coefficient γ controls the adaptive\ngain; γ = 0 recovers the fixed-strength intervention.\n\nTurn-Boundary Subspace and\nRealization-Aware Gating\nPredicted length.\nturn’s log length\n\nThe controller regresses the follower\n\nLt+1 = log(1 + words(ut+1 )) .\n\n\fPredicted Move\nacknowledge\ncheck\nexplain\nquery\nreply\n\nMove Steering\napply\napply\napply\napply\nwithhold\n\nEOT Steering\napply\nwithhold\nwithhold\napply\napply\n\nDataset\n\nPlurality Unanimous\nAgreement Agreement\n\nMapTask\nFindTask\nCReST\n\n76.4%\n67.8%\n75.5%\n\n94.6%\n91.9%\n86.5%\n\nOverall\n\n73.9%\n\n90.5%\n\nTable 11: Realization-aware gating policy.\nAt inference, the prediction\n⊤\nb t+1 = wL\nL\not\n\nis mapped to the turn-boundary coefficient β by a monotone\ncalibration fitted on training dialogues.\n\nHuman Study\nWe conduct a human study to validate the automatic move\nclassifier against human judgment on model-generated responses, and to measure agreement between human annotators and the LLM-based evaluator. From the pooled generations of all methods, we sample 500 responses—100 for each\nof the five target moves, distributed approximately equally\nacross the three generation models and three datasets. Each\nresponse is annotated by three workers who see only the dialogue context and the generated utterance and select one\nconversational move; the target move, generation method,\nand classifier prediction are withheld so that annotation is\nblind.\nAnnotators. All annotations were collected on Amazon\nMechanical Turk (AMT) using workers with a minimum\napproval rate of 95%. Annotators were restricted to Englishspeaking countries to ensure reliable comprehension. Compensation was set to meet or exceed the U.S. federal minimum wage based on conservative task-time estimates. No\npersonally identifiable information was collected.\n\nHuman–LLM Agreement\nWe compare human judgments to the LLM move classifier\nat the item level. Human decisions are aggregated by plurality voting across the three annotators, and the LLM prediction is the label assigned by the two-stage Qwen2.5-72BInstruct classifier (Yang et al. 2025). Agreement is reported\nper dataset in Table 12: overall, the classifier matches the\nhuman plurality on 73.9% of items, rising to 90.5% on the\n327 items where all three annotators agreed. Inter-annotator\nagreement is moderate (Fleiss’ κ = 0.682 overall), and the\nclassifier’s agreement with the human majority is comparable\nto the agreement among annotators themselves.\nTo assess alignment at the distribution level, we compare\nthe classifier’s normalized move probabilities with the human\nvote shares across all item–move pairs. The two are positively correlated (Pearson r = 0.721 overall, ranging from\nr = 0.62 for explain to r = 0.85 for query), indicating that\nthe classifier’s confidence tracks human uncertainty. Moreover, ranking the seven generation methods by human-judged\ntarget-move realization reproduces the classifier-based ranking almost exactly (Spearman ρ = 0.96, Pearson r = 0.99),\n\nTable 12: Agreement between the automatic classifier and\nhuman judgments. Plurality agreement uses items with a\nclear majority; unanimous agreement uses only items on\nwhich all three annotators selected the same move.\n\nshowing that the two evaluators agree not only per item but\nalso in their relative assessment of methods. These results\nsupport the use of the LLM move classifier as a scalable\nproxy for human judgment in our evaluation.\n\nMTurk Interfaces\nFigure 5 shows the annotation interface presented to workers.\n\n\fFigure 5: Human annotation interface for move classification. Annotators are shown the preceding dialogue context and a single\ncandidate response, and asked to select the conversational move the response performs (acknowledge, check, explain, query, or\nreply). The target move, generation method, and classifier prediction are hidden.\n\n\f", "numLines": 686, "startLine": 1200, "totalLines": 1885}}}
